{"body": "I take the P7/P9 relationship to be more specific than “criticisms can target different things.” P7 gives a taxonomy of targets; P9 supplies a conditional objection whose target is not fixed by its wording. My proposal is to treat an account of global availability as having a **claim strength** as well as a computation. Once that is explicit, P9’s compatibility objection can be located in P7’s sense, and the question of whether it requires a change becomes conditional rather than automatic.\n\nI will call the proposal a **target-indexed availability account**. It is my construction, not something C0 states. It has five distinguishable parts:\n\n1. **Local layer L**: for each booking, the set of possible reservations under the chosen reading of supersession, expiry, release, and ties. P9’s “unresolved alternatives” live here.\n2. **Aggregation layer A**: the map from local alternatives to a global representation. It may sum, take per-booking minima and maxima, build an interval, or build a set of combinations.\n3. **Constraint layer C**: the global constraints the account actually models. Capacity is one. A shared physical resource is not named in the snapshot, so it is absent unless added.\n4. **Claim layer R**: what the global output is asserted to be. It might be only an arithmetic summary, a bound, a feasible set, or a decision-grade answer.\n5. **Target register T**: P7’s distinction made operational. A criticism is labelled as targeting a rule, a record, a booking history, the snapshot as a whole, an assumption, an aggregation map, a constraint model, or a projection.\n\nOn this account, P9’s compatibility objection says: if booking A’s high alternative and booking B’s high alternative are individually possible under L, but they share an unnamed physical resource that makes the combination impossible, then an interval built from per-booking min/max may include a joint combination that is not jointly realizable. That objection does not attack L. It attacks A’s independence assumption, C’s omission, and/or R’s overclaim. In P7’s terms, it is closest to a criticism of an assumption—specifically a capacity-only or independence assumption—or a criticism of the snapshot as a whole for incompleteness. P7’s explicit assumption example is “the assumption that logical time is meaningful”; the resource-model assumption is an extension of that target category, not a quotation from P7.\n\nThe target of P9’s objection is therefore **claim-relative**. If the account claims only that it exposes local alternatives, the objection is a criticism of the snapshot’s completeness: the snapshot does not name the shared resource. If the account claims that its min/max interval is the feasible range of global availability, the objection is a criticism of the aggregation rule: it assumes independent combinability. If the account claims that its interval is decision-grade, the objection is a criticism of the projection policy: it hides correlations that may matter downstream. This target-shifting reading is my addition. P9 contains the compatibility possibility; P7 contains the target distinction; the connection is that P9’s objection lands on different targets depending on what the global account asserts.\n\nDoes P9’s objection require a change? Conditionally. If the account already labels its global output as “capacity minus per-booking min/max under independent local alternatives; joint realizability is not asserted,” then no computational change is required. The objection is already contained as a caveat, though it may still criticize downstream uses that treat the interval as feasible. If the account says the interval is the range of possible global availabilities, or is sufficient for allocation decisions, then yes: either add a constraint model or weaken the claim. The minimal change is not to alter L or the supersession rule. It is to add a marker such as “constraints modeled: capacity only; cross-booking correlations: not represented.” A stronger change is to replace the interval with a set of possible worlds plus a compatibility relation.\n\nThere is also a criticism internal to P9 that I think the pairing makes visible. P9 says that “the best one can do is expose the unresolved alternatives rather than pretend to a single answer.” But exposing alternatives per booking does not by itself expose cross-booking incompatibilities. If an account exposes L and then presents A as if it covered all combinations subject only to capacity, it can still pretend to more than it has. So the compatibility objection rebounds on P9’s own remedy: local exposure is not global exposure. This is a criticism of a reconstruction/aggregation rule—the rule that exposing local alternatives is sufficient—not of the local alternatives themselves. P9 contains both the compatibility objection and the exposure conjecture, but it does not explicitly apply the former to the latter. That application is my addition.\n\nA discriminating question follows: take an account of global availability. Does adding a resource constraint that leaves every booking’s local alternative set and per-booking minima/maxima unchanged but removes some joint combinations change the account’s global output? If yes, the account represents correlations. If no, it is only an independent projection. P9’s objection is decisive only for accounts that need the yes-case but supply the no-case. The additional observation that would matter is whether such shared resources exist, whether capacity is a scalar or a resource vector, and whether downstream decisions require a feasible set or only a conservative summary.\n\nThe selected E009 occurrence is not additional warehouse evidence; I treat it as a criticism artifact. It can illustrate a target—language limitation—but it cannot decide whether a shared resource exists or whether P9’s objection requires a change. Its claims about a formal language failing to represent correlations are not evidence that the warehouse has correlations.\n\nWhat remains unresolved: P9’s incompatibility is a conditional possibility, not an observation. I do not claim the missing resource exists. I do not claim P7 anticipated resource modelling. I do not claim exposing alternatives is wrong; I claim its sufficiency is disputed. The next question is: should the target of the compatibility objection be recorded as a missing resource model in the snapshot, or as an independence assumption in the aggregation rule? That distinction determines whether the required change is a data criticism, a rule criticism, or only a caveat on the claim.", "commitments": "I take the P7/P9 relationship to be more specific than “criticisms can target different things.” P7 gives a taxonomy of targets; P9 supplies a conditional objection whose target is not fixed by its wording. My proposal is to treat an account of global availability as having a **claim strength** as well as a computation. Once that is explicit, P9’s compatibility objection can be located in P7’s sense, and the question of whether it requires a change becomes conditional rather than automatic.\n\nI will call the proposal a **target-indexed availability account**. It is my construction, not something C0 states. It has five distinguishable parts:\n\n1. **Local layer L**: for each booking, the set of possible reservations under the chosen reading of supersession, expiry, release, and ties. P9’s “unresolved alternatives” live here.\n2. **Aggregation layer A**: the map from local alternatives to a global representation. It may sum, take per-booking minima and maxima, build an interval, or build a set of combinations.\n3. **Constraint layer C**: the global constraints the account actually models. Capacity is one. A shared physical resource is not named in the snapshot, so it is absent unless added.\n4. **Claim layer R**: what the global output is asserted to be. It might be only an arithmetic summary, a bound, a feasible set, or a decision-grade answer.