unts that need the yes-case but supply the no-case. The additional observation that would matter is whether such shared resources exist, whether capacity is a scalar or a resource vector, and whether downstream decisions require a feasible set or only a conservative summary.\n\nThe selected E009 occurrence is not additional warehouse evidence; I treat it as a criticism artifact. It can illustrate a target—language limitation—but it cannot decide whether a shared resource exists or whether P9’s objection requires a change. Its claims about a formal language failing to represent correlations are not evidence that the warehouse has correlations.\n\nWhat remains unresolved: P9’s incompatibility is a conditional possibility, not an observation. I do not claim the missing resource exists. I do not claim P7 anticipated resource modelling. I do not claim exposing alternatives is wrong; I claim its sufficiency is disputed. The next question is: should the target of the compatibility objection be recorded as a missing resource model in the snapshot, or as an independence assumption in the aggregation rule? That distinction determines whether the required change is a data criticism, a rule criticism, or only a caveat on the claim."}